---------------------
merchant-analytics:
    🎯 How to present in interviews
    You can summarize like this:
    “At Paytm, I worked on Merchant Analytics, a SaaS-based analytics platform that provided merchants with actionable insights across four key areas
        — Payment Source Analysis, Success Rate Analysis, Customer Analysis, and Industry vs You.
    It helped merchants monitor payment reliability, customer behavior, and industry benchmarks, helping merchants improve conversion rates, retention, and revenue visibility.
    I was responsible for designing backend modules for data ingestion, aggregation, and real-time metric computation using Java, Spring Boot, and distributed data stores.”

    💳 1. Payment Source Analysis

    Purpose:
    To help merchants understand which payment instruments their customers are using — such as UPI, wallets, credit/debit cards, or net banking — and how each performs over time.

    Use Case:
    Merchants can see what percentage of their transactions come from each payment source, along with metrics like transaction count, volume, and average order value.
    This helps them optimize payment method availability, negotiate better rates, or target promotions (e.g., UPI cashback vs card offers).

    Example Insight:

    “UPI transactions grew 25% MoM while card usage dropped 10% — indicating a shift in customer payment preference.”

    Business Value:
    Improves conversion rates and payment experience by identifying the most preferred and reliable payment methods for customers.

    ⸻

    📈 2. Success Rate Analysis

    Purpose:
    To provide merchants visibility into payment success/failure rates across different payment methods, banks, and gateways.

    Use Case:
    Helps merchants and internal Paytm ops teams pinpoint failure reasons — like network issues, bank downtime, or gateway bottlenecks — and take proactive steps.

    Example Insight:

    “HDFC Bank’s UPI success rate dropped to 92% between 2–3 PM — leading to 5% drop in checkout conversions.”

    Business Value:
    Improves transaction reliability, reduces payment drop-offs, and builds trust with end users by identifying and mitigating issues quickly.

    ⸻

    👥 3. Customer Analysis

    Purpose:
    To give merchants a 360° view of their customer base — behavior, spending patterns, frequency, and demographics.

    Use Case:
    Merchants can segment customers into cohorts — new, returning, loyal, or dormant — and analyze metrics like average transaction value, purchase frequency, or recency.

    Example Insight:

    “40% of transactions are from repeat customers; high-value users contribute 60% of total volume.”

    Business Value:
    Empowers merchants to run targeted marketing campaigns, loyalty programs, and customer retention strategies based on data-backed insights.

    ⸻

    🏭 4. Industry vs You Analysis

    Purpose:
    To benchmark a merchant’s performance against industry averages or peer merchants in similar categories (e.g., food delivery, retail, travel).

    Use Case:
    Allows merchants to understand whether their success rate, customer retention, or payment mix is better or worse than industry peers.

    Example Insight:

    “Your average transaction success rate (94%) is higher than industry average (91%), but repeat customer ratio is 10% lower.”

    Business Value:
    Drives strategic decisions and helps merchants identify gaps in performance, set realistic goals, and prioritize areas for improvement.
